  "historical_output_text": " One familiar weather pattern is a warm front, which can bring changes in temperature and weather conditions. Imagine you're standing in a field and you suddenly notice the temperature starts to rise and the sky turns grey. This is because a warm front is moving in.\n\nA warm front happens when a warm air mass moves into an area that's cooler. The warm air is less dense, so it rides up over the cooler air, creating a slope in the atmosphere. As this warm air rises, it cools down, causing water vapor to condense into clouds. This is why you see a blanket of grey clouds forming and why the temperature increases.\n\nAs the warm air continues to rise and cool, it can lead to the formation of precipitation if the air is humid enough. So, you might start to see rain or drizzle. Sometimes, the air can rise so much that it forms",
